+
+## Statement
+
+Let $A$ be a framed unitary ASD connection on the round oriented $S^4$, let $\mathcal E$ be its Ward bundle on $Z=\mathbb CP^3$, and let $k=-q_N(A)$. Assume the conditional Penrose-transform input stated in the next paragraph for both $\mathcal E$ and $\mathcal E^\vee$. Assume also the exact projective GAGA and line-family base-change inputs needed to regard $\mathcal E$ as an algebraic bundle, to make its restrictions trivial on a complex-open neighbourhood of at least one real twistor line, and to apply the diagonal resolution. Finally, use the Chern-character normalization $\operatorname{ch}(\mathcal E)=N-kH^2$ in $A^*(\mathbb CP^3)_\mathbb Q$, where $H=c_1(\mathcal O(1))$; this normalization is conditional on the twistor pullback class convention matching the framed charge.
+
+Under these inputs,
+
+$$H^1(Z,\mathcal E(-2))=H^1(Z,\mathcal E^\vee(-2))=0.$$
+
+There is a canonical Beilinson three-term monad
+
+$$\mathcal O_Z(-1)\otimes U\longrightarrow\mathcal O_Z\otimes K\longrightarrow\mathcal O_Z(1)\otimes U',$$
+
+whose middle cohomology is $\mathcal E$ and whose terms are
+
+$$U=H^2(Z,\mathcal E(-3)),\qquad K=H^2(Z,\mathcal E\otimes\Omega_Z^2),\qquad U'=H^2(Z,\mathcal E\otimes\Omega_Z^1(-1)),$$
+
+with $\dim U=\dim U'=k$ and $\dim K=2k+N$. The construction is natural in framed bundle isomorphisms and unique up to changes of bases in $U,K,U'$. If the ASD connection has a parallel subbundle, it splits orthogonally as a trivial flat summand and a summand with no nonzero parallel sections; the monad construction is additive across this splitting and uses no hypothesis $H^0(Z,\mathcal E)=0$.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[[thm-qftx-framed-penrose-ward-correspondence-on-s4]]
+
+[[lem-qftx-asd-penrose-kernel-vanishing]]
+
+[[lem-qftx-twistor-line-section-direct-image]]
+
+[[thm-qftx-projective-gaga-for-monad-data]]
+
+[Donaldson, §5, printed pp. 8–10](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.08639)
+
+[Nakajima–Yoshioka, §3.1, printed pp. 17–19](https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0311058)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The Ward bundle and its framed ASD connection, the round twistor conventions, the conditional Penrose maps, the projective-family hypotheses, and the stated Chern-character normalization.
+
+[A1] The Penrose maps $H^1(Z,\mathcal E(-2))\to\ker(\nabla_A^*\nabla_A+R/8)$ and the analogous map for $\mathcal E^\vee$ are injective. This is the exact analytic input in [[lem-qftx-asd-penrose-kernel-vanishing]]; the transform itself is not proved there.
+
+[A2] Analytification and the universal line-family base-change maps have the exact GAGA hypotheses recorded in [[thm-qftx-projective-gaga-for-monad-data]] and the conditional local result [[lem-qftx-twistor-line-section-direct-image]]. The batch-59 GAGA carrier exists but its current Step 1 record is escalated on missing analytic and relative-comparison proofs; surjectivity at a real-line fibre remains an explicit assumption of the local direct-image lemma.
+
+[A3] The twistor pullback of the framed Chern class has $c_1(\mathcal E)=0$, $c_2(\mathcal E)=kH^2$, and $c_3(\mathcal E)=0$, so $\operatorname{ch}(\mathcal E)=N-kH^2$. The $U(N)$ determinant and charge calculation is supplied by [[lem-qftx-u-n-determinant-flat-charge-normalization]]; the equality $\pi^*u=H^2$ for the orientation-fixed twistor fibration is part of the conditional normalization in the Statement and remains to be checked against the GAGA/twistor interface.
+
+[F1] The round scalar curvature satisfies $R>0$, and a smooth section solving $(\nabla^*\nabla+R/8)s=0$ vanishes by the positive-kernel argument. [[lem-qftx-asd-penrose-kernel-vanishing]], [[lem-qftx-round-s4-positive-scalar-kernel-vanishes]]
+
+[F2] For a flat connection on the simply connected $S^4$, parallel transport gives a global parallel frame; for a unitary connection the orthogonal complement of its parallel sections is preserved. [[thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected]], [[thm-a-flat-connection-admits-local-parallel-frames]], [[prop-flat-connections-have-locally-path-independent-parallel-transport-on-a-coordinate-ball]]
+
+[F3] The standard projective diagonal has the Koszul resolution
+
+$$0\to\mathcal O(-3)\boxtimes\Omega^3(3)\to\mathcal O(-2)\boxtimes\Omega^2(2)\to\mathcal O(-1)\boxtimes\Omega^1(1)\to\mathcal O\to\mathcal O_\Delta\to0,$$
+
+because the diagonal is a regular embedding of codimension three and its three local coordinate differences form a regular sequence. [[lem-koszul-resolution-and-flat-fibre-restriction]]
+
+[F4] The bounded hypercohomology spectral sequence has first page $E_1^{p,q}=H^q(K^p)$, differential $d_r:(p,q)\mapsto(p+r,q-r+1)$, and converges with a finite filtration to the hypercohomology of the diagonal complex. [[thm-first-hypercohomology-spectral-sequence]]
+
+[F5] On $\mathbb P^3$, Serre duality identifies $H^q(F)^*$ with $\operatorname{Ext}^{3-q}(F,\mathcal O(-4))$; for locally free $F$ this is $H^{3-q}(F^*(-4))$. [[thm-serre-duality-projective-space-coherent-sheaves]]
+
+[F6] On $\mathbb P^n$, $H^0(\mathcal O(d))=0$ for $d<0$, and the only possible nonzero cohomology of $\mathcal O(d)$ is in degrees $0$ and $n$, with $H^n(\mathcal O(d))=0$ for $d>-n-1$. [[thm-cohomology-projective-space-twisting-sheaves]]
+
+[F7] Hirzebruch–Riemann–Roch on $\mathbb P^3$ gives $\chi(F)=\int_{\mathbb P^3}\operatorname{ch}(F)\operatorname{td}(T\mathbb P^3)$ for the vector bundles and twists in this argument. [[thm-rr-for-projective-space-projections]]
+
+## Proof
+
+**Given:** [A1]–[A3] and [F1]–[F7].
+
+1.1 By [A1] and [F1], the image of every class in $H^1(Z,\mathcal E(-2))$ is a solution of the coupled Laplace equation with positive scalar term, hence is zero; injectivity makes the class zero. Applying the same argument to $\mathcal E^\vee$ gives $H^1(Z,\mathcal E^\vee(-2))=0$. This argument includes reducible connections. [A1, F1, algebra]
+
+1.2 Let $L\cong\mathbb P^1$ be a real twistor line. Its normal bundle in $\mathbb P^3$ is $\mathcal O(1)^{\oplus2}$, and the conormal sequence splits because its extension group is $H^1(\mathcal O(1))^{\oplus2}=0$. Hence $\Omega_Z^1|_L\cong\mathcal O(-2)\oplus\mathcal O(-1)^{\oplus2}$ and $T_Z|_L\cong\mathcal O(2)\oplus\mathcal O(1)^{\oplus2}$. Since $\Omega_Z^2\cong T_Z(-4)$, each of $\mathcal E(-3)|_L$, $\mathcal E(-2)|_L$, $(\mathcal E\otimes\Omega_Z^1(-1))|_L$, and $(\mathcal E\otimes\Omega_Z^2)|_L$ is a sum of negative line bundles. The Serre-dual bundles restrict respectively to $\mathcal E^*(-1)$, $\mathcal E^*(-2)$, $\mathcal E^*\otimes T_Z(-3)$, and $\mathcal E^*\otimes\Omega_Z^1$, also sums of negative line bundles. Thus [F6] gives $H^0(Z,F)=0$ and [F5] gives $H^3(Z,F)=0$ for all four coefficient bundles $F$. [A2, F5, F6, algebra]
+
+2.1 Choose a plane $P\cong\mathbb P^2$ containing a real twistor line. By [A2] and the direct-image result, an open family of lines near that line has evaluation isomorphisms and hence trivial restrictions of $\mathcal E$. Those lines form a nonempty open subset of the line family of $P$; the incidence projection from this open subset covers a dense subset of $P$. A section of $\mathcal E(-2)|_P$ vanishes on every such line because $H^0(\mathbb P^1,\mathcal O(-2)^{\oplus N})=0$, so it vanishes on a dense subset and is zero. The divisor sequence $0\to\mathcal E(-3)\to\mathcal E(-2)\to\mathcal E(-2)|_P\to0$ and step 1.1 now give $H^1(Z,\mathcal E(-3))=0$. [A2, F6, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+2.2 Tensor $0\to\Omega_Z^1(-1)\to\mathcal O(-2)^{\oplus4}\to\mathcal O(-1)\to0$ by $\mathcal E$. Step 1.2 gives $H^0(\mathcal E(-2))=H^0(\mathcal E(-1))=0$, and step 1.1 gives $H^1(\mathcal E(-2))=0$. The long exact sequence yields $H^1(\mathcal E\otimes\Omega_Z^1(-1))=0$. Serre duality and step 1.1 also give $H^2(\mathcal E(-2))=0$. [F5, F6, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]
+
+3.1 Apply [F4] to the diagonal Koszul resolution [F3], tensoring by $\mathcal E(-2)$ and twisting the output factor by $\mathcal O(2)$. Its first page is $E_1^{p,q}=H^q(Z,\mathcal E(-2)\otimes\Omega_Z^{-p}(-p))\otimes\mathcal O_Z(p+2)$ for $p=-3,-2,-1,0$. Steps 1.2, 2.1 and 2.2 and Serre duality make every term in rows $q=0,1,3$ vanish except possibly $H^1(\mathcal E\otimes\Omega_Z^2)$ at $(-2,1)$. No differential can enter this term; its only possible outgoing $d_2$ lands in $H^0(\mathcal E(-2))=0$. It therefore survives to $E_\infty$, but the diagonal complex resolves $\mathcal E(-2)$ in degree zero and has no degree $-1$ hypercohomology. Since $(-2)+1=-1$, the term is zero. Thus the remaining spectral-sequence row is $q=2$. [F3, F4, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 2.2, algebra]
+
+4.1 The row $q=2$ gives the complex $H^2(\mathcal E(-3))\otimes\mathcal O(-1)\to H^2(\mathcal E\otimes\Omega_Z^2)\otimes\mathcal O\to H^2(\mathcal E\otimes\Omega_Z^1(-1))\otimes\mathcal O(1)$. All higher differentials vanish because all other rows are zero. Its only cohomology is the degree-zero hypercohomology of the diagonal resolution, namely $\mathcal E$; the first and last terms are exact because the other total degrees vanish. The maps are linear in the homogeneous output coordinates, since each Koszul differential is contraction with the universal linear section. Thus this is the stated monad; its cohomology vector spaces are canonical, and changing bases acts by $GL(U)\times GL(K)\times GL(U')$. [F3, F4, step 3.1, algebra]
+
+4.2 From [A3], $\operatorname{ch}(\mathcal E(t))=Ne^{tH}-kH^2e^{tH}$ and $\operatorname{td}(T_Z)=1+2H+\tfrac{11}{6}H^2+H^3$. Hirzebruch–Riemann–Roch [F7] gives $\chi(\mathcal E(t))=N\binom{t+3}{3}-k(t+2)$, with values $k,-N+2k,0,-k$ for $t=-3,-4,-2,-1$. The Euler sequences $0\to\mathcal O(-4)\to\mathcal O(-3)^{\oplus4}\to\Omega_Z^2\to0$ and $0\to\Omega_Z^1(-1)\to\mathcal O(-2)^{\oplus4}\to\mathcal O(-1)\to0$ give Euler characteristics $k,2k+N,k$ for the three monad coefficient bundles. Steps 1.2, 2.1, 2.2 and 3.1 leave only $H^2$ in each, so these values are the dimensions. [A3, F6, F7, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 2.2, step 3.1, algebra]
+
+5.1 The space of parallel sections of $\widehat E$ evaluates to a trivial subbundle, and its orthogonal complement is parallel because the connection is unitary. No nonzero parallel section lies in the complement. Every flat summand is trivial since $S^4$ is simply connected [F2]. The Ward transform and diagonal construction preserve direct sums. For a trivial rank-$m$ summand $\mathcal O_Z^{\oplus m}$, the two Euler sequences and [F6] give coefficient groups $H^2(\mathcal O(-3))=0$, $H^2(\Omega_Z^2)^{\oplus m}\cong\mathbb C^m$, and $H^2(\Omega_Z^1(-1))=0$, so its monad is $0\to\mathcal O_Z^{\oplus m}\to0$. Flat summands are therefore included without imposing $H^0(Z,\mathcal E)=0$ or $H^0(Z,\mathcal E^\vee)=0$. No Dirac index or kernel-dimension formula is used. [F2, F6, step 4.1, algebra] ∎
